      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "For $f\\in H$, put $f_m=f\\mathbf1_{[-m,m]}$. Then $f_m\\in D(Q)$ because $|x|\\le m$ on its support, and $f_m\\to f$ in $L^2$ by dominated convergence applied to $|f_m-f|^2\\le |f|^2$; hence $D(Q)$ is dense. For $f,g\\in D(Q)$, $xf\\overline g$ is integrable by Cauchy–Schwarz, and real-valuedness of $x$ gives $\\langle Qf,g\\rangle=\\langle f,Qg\\rangle$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "Equip $\\mathcal G=\\{f:\\int_{\\mathbb R}(1+x^2)|f(x)|^2dx<\\infty\\}$ with the explicit weighted inner product $\\langle f,g\\rangle_{\\mathcal G}:=\\int_{\\mathbb R}(1+x^2)f(x)\\overline{g(x)}\\,dx$. Multiplication by $\\sqrt{1+x^2}$ is an onto isometry from $\\mathcal G$ to $L^2(\\mathbb R)$, with inverse multiplication by $(1+x^2)^{-1/2}$, so $\\mathcal G$ is Hilbert. Its norm equals $\\|f\\|_Q$ on $D(Q)$. Suppose $f\\in\\mathcal G$ is orthogonal to every $g_n$ in this weighted inner product. Since the $H_n$ have successive degrees and nonzero leading coefficients [F2], their finite span is all polynomials. Therefore $h(x)=(1+x^2)f(x)g_0(x)$ lies in $L^1$ by Cauchy–Schwarz and $\\int h(x)x^jdx=0$ for every $j\\ge0$.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F5",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Let $g\\in D(Q^*)$ and write $k=Q^*g$. For each $m$, the vector $f_m=(xg-k)\\mathbf1_{[-m,m]}$ belongs to $D(Q)$ since $x$ is bounded there and $g,k\\in L^2$. The adjoint identity for $f_m$ yields $0=\\langle Qf_m,g\\rangle-\\langle f_m,k\\rangle =\\int_{-m}^m|xg-k|^2\\,dx$. Thus $xg=k$ almost everywhere on every $[-m,m]$, hence on $\\mathbb R$; since $k\\in L^2$, $g\\in D(Q)$ and $Q^*g=Qg$. Conversely, symmetry in step 1.1 gives $D(Q)\\subseteq D(Q^*)$ and $Q^*f=Qf$. Therefore $Q=Q^*$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "Fix $\\xi\\in\\mathbb R$ and let $S_N(x)=\\sum_{j=0}^N(-2\\pi i\\xi x)^j/j!$. By [F4], $S_N(x)\\to e^{-2\\pi i\\xi x}$ and $|S_N(x)|\\le e^{2\\pi|\\xi||x|}$. The majorant $|h(x)|e^{2\\pi|\\xi||x|}$ is integrable: Cauchy–Schwarz bounds its integral by $\\|f\\|_{\\mathcal G}\\bigl(\\int(1+x^2)g_0(x)^2e^{4\\pi|\\xi||x|}dx\\bigr)^{1/2}$, and the latter integral is finite because completing the square gives $-x^2+4\\pi|\\xi||x|=-(|x|-2\\pi|\\xi|)^2+(2\\pi|\\xi|)^2$, so [F5] applies. Dominated convergence and the vanishing moments from step 1.2 imply $\\widehat h(\\xi)=\\lim_N\\int hS_N=0$. Since $\\xi$ was arbitrary, $\\widehat h$ vanishes identically.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.2",
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Fourier uniqueness [F3] gives $h=0$ almost everywhere. Since $1+x^2>0$ and $g_0(x)>0$ for every $x$, this implies $f=0$ in $\\mathcal G$. Thus the orthogonal complement of the Hermite span in $\\mathcal G$ is zero; [F6] makes that span dense in $\\mathcal G$. Because the $\\mathcal G$ norm is the graph norm of $Q$, the Hermite span is a graph core, proving the statement.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.2",
            "step 2.2",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F6"
          ]
        }
