      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Measurable representatives may be chosen for $f,f',g,g'$. Replacing a\nrepresentative on a null set changes its product only on a product-null set:\ncover the other sigma-finite factor by countably many finite-measure sets and\napply the rectangle formula [F1] on each. Thus the product rule is well\ndefined on $L^2$ classes. By [F7], $f\\overline{f'}$ and $g\\overline{g'}$\nare integrable; Fubini [F2] gives the product norm\n$\\|f(x)g(y)\\|_2^2=\\|f\\|_2^2\\|g\\|_2^2$, so each elementary product belongs\nto the target space. It also gives\n$$\\langle f(x)g(y),f'(x)g'(y)\\rangle_{L^2(X\\times Y)} =\\langle f,f'\\rangle_{H_X}\\langle g,g'\\rangle_{H_Y}.$$\nBy sesquilinearity, the rule $\\sum_j\\alpha_j f_j\\otimes g_j\\mapsto\n\\sum_j\\alpha_j f_j(x)g_j(y)$ preserves inner products on finite sums. If two\ntensor presentations define the same algebraic tensor, their difference has\nzero tensor norm by [F5], hence its product function has zero $L^2$ norm; the\nrule is well-defined on the algebraic tensor product and extends uniquely as\nan isometry $T$ to the completion.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F5",
            "F7",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Choose sigma-finite exhaustions $X_k\\uparrow X$ and $Y_k\\uparrow Y$ with finite measure, and put $Z_k=X_k\\times Y_k$. By the rectangle formula [F1], $(\\mu\\times\\nu)(Z_k)<\\infty$, and $Z_k\\uparrow X\\times Y$. For fixed $k$, let $\\mathcal C_k$ be the family of product-measurable $E\\subseteq Z_k$ such that $\\mathbf1_E$ belongs to the closed range of $T$. It contains $Z_k$, since $\\mathbf1_{Z_k}=T(\\mathbf1_{X_k}\\otimes\\mathbf1_{Y_k})$. If $E\\subseteq F$ are in $\\mathcal C_k$, then $\\mathbf1_{F\\setminus E}=\\mathbf1_F-\\mathbf1_E$ is in that closed range. If $E_j\\uparrow E$ in $Z_k$, then $\\|\\mathbf1_E-\\mathbf1_{E_j}\\|_2^2=(\\mu\\times\\nu)(E\\setminus E_j)\\to0$ by [F6], so $E\\in\\mathcal C_k$. Thus $\\mathcal C_k$ is a lambda-system on $Z_k$. For every measurable rectangle $A\\times B$, its restriction to $Z_k$ is $(A\\cap X_k)\\times(B\\cap Y_k)$, whose indicator is the image of $\\mathbf1_{A\\cap X_k}\\otimes\\mathbf1_{B\\cap Y_k}$; these restricted rectangles are a generating pi-system by [F4]. Dynkin's theorem [F4] gives $\\mathbf1_E\\in\\overline{\\operatorname{ran}T}$ for every product-measurable $E\\subseteq Z_k$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "step 1.1",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Let $E$ be any completed-product-measurable set with finite product measure. By the completion definition [F1], it differs from a product-measurable set $E_0$ by a subset of a product-null set, so $\\mathbf1_E=\\mathbf1_{E_0}$ as $L^2$ classes. Since $Z_k\\uparrow X\\times Y$, [F6] gives $(\\mu\\times\\nu)(E_0\\setminus Z_k)\\to0$, so $\\mathbf1_{E_0}=\\lim_k\\mathbf1_{E_0\\cap Z_k}$ in $L^2$; step 2.1 puts each truncated indicator in the closed range of $T$. Every finite-measure simple function for the completed measure is a finite linear combination of indicators of completed-measurable finite-measure sets; replacing each set by its product-measurable representative gives the same $L^2$ class. Such simple functions are dense by [F3]. Consequently the range of $T$ is dense in the full target $L^2$ space. Since the range of an isometry from a complete space is closed, $T$ is onto and therefore unitary. Its uniqueness follows because elementary tensors span a dense subspace.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
