          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-nrqm-composites-permutation-sector-projections",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $H$ be a complex Hilbert space, $n\\ge1$, and $H^{\\otimes n}$ the $n$-fold completed tensor",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For an integer $d\\ge1$, let $\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^d)$ be the complex Schwartz space with seminorms $p_{\\alpha\\beta}(f)=\\sup_{x\\in\\mathbb R^d}|x^\\alpha\\partial^\\beta f(x)|$ for all multi-indices $\\alpha,\\beta$;",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Fix $k,m\\ge0$, write $n=k+m$, and let $\\psi_m\\in K_s^{(m)}$. Expanding\nthe permutation average and using symmetry of $\\psi_m$ gives\n$P_{s,n}(e^{\\otimes k}\\otimes\\psi_m)=\\binom nk^{-1}\\sum_{\\substack{I\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}\\\\|I|=k}}v_I$, where $v_I$ is the tensor with $e$ in the slots in $I$ and the $m$ factors of\n$\\psi_m$ in the remaining slots, symmetrized there. Indeed, for each fixed\nplacement $I$, the $k!m!$ permutations of the equal $e$ factors and the\nsymmetric $\\psi_m$ give the same tensor, and\n$k!m!/n!=\\binom nk^{-1}$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "construct",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Distinct placements $v_I$ are orthogonal: if $I\\ne I'$, some slot contains $e$ in one tensor and a vector of $K$ in the other, and $e\\perp K$. Each $v_I$ has norm $\\|\\psi_m\\|$, so step 1.1 gives $\\|P_{s,n}(e^{\\otimes k}\\otimes\\psi_m)\\|^2 =\\binom nk^{-1}\\|\\psi_m\\|^2$. The same orthogonality shows that the images for different $k$ in a fixed $n$ sector are orthogonal. Thus the factor $\\sqrt{\\binom nk}$ makes $J_{k,m}$ isometric and their sector ranges are orthogonal.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Algebraic elementary tensors in $H^{\\widehat\\otimes n}$ are dense by [F1]. Expand each factor using $H=\\mathbb Ce\\oplus K$, distribute the finite tensor product, and apply $P_{s,n}$. Every resulting term has exactly $k$ factors $e$ and $m=n-k$ factors in $K$, so it lies in the range described in step 2.1. Since $P_{s,n}$ is bounded and algebraic elementary tensors are dense, their symmetrizations are dense in $H_s^{(n)}$; the range of the isometry is closed, hence it equals $H_s^{(n)}$. Taking the orthogonal direct sum over $n=k+m$ proves that $J$ is onto and unitary.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "On a vector $e^{\\otimes k}\\otimes\\psi_m$ with $n=k+m$, creation by $e$ acts by $\\sqrt{n+1}P_{s,n+1}(e\\otimes J_{k,m}(e^{\\otimes k}\\otimes\\psi_m))$. Since $P_{s,n+1}$ averages over all permutations, symmetrizing the last $n$ slots first does not change the result. This equals $\\sqrt{n+1}\\sqrt{\\binom nk}P_{s,n+1}(e^{\\otimes(k+1)}\\otimes\\psi_m)$. The binomial identity $\\binom{n+1}{k+1}=\\frac{n+1}{k+1}\\binom nk$ makes this exactly $J_{k+1,m}(\\sqrt{k+1}\\,e^{\\otimes(k+1)}\\otimes\\psi_m)$. Thus creation becomes the stated one-mode raising map. Annihilation by $e$ is zero for $k=0$, because every first tensor factor lies in $K$. For $k\\ge1$, the placement formula of step 1.1 shows that contraction keeps exactly the $\\binom{n-1}{k-1}$ placements with $e$ in the contracted slot. Therefore $a(e)J_{k,m}\\psi_m= \\sqrt n\\sqrt{\\binom nk}\\frac{\\binom{n-1}{k-1}}{\\binom nk} P_{s,n-1}(e^{\\otimes(k-1)}\\otimes\\psi_m) =\\sqrt k\\,J_{k-1,m}\\psi_m$, using $\\binom nk=\\frac nk\\binom{n-1}{k-1}$. Thus annihilation becomes the stated one-mode lowering map and leaves the $K$ factor unchanged.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
