      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "For each $n\\ge1$, iterate the product-tensor unitary of [F2]; induction\non $n$ gives a unitary from $H^{\\widehat\\otimes n}$ to the full completed\nproduct $L^2(X^n)$. On elementary tensors the permutation $U_\\pi$ becomes\ncoordinate permutation, so by density the unitary intertwines $P_{s,n}$ with\naveraging over coordinate permutations [F3]. Its restriction identifies\n$H_s^{(n)}$ with the closed symmetric subspace. The $n=0$ sector is $\\mathbb C$\nby definition. Each $s_n$ is measurable as a finite sum of measurable\ncoordinate maps and is invariant under all permutations.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "given",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Let $\\Omega=\\bigsqcup_{n\\ge0}X^n$ be the countable disjoint union, with the completed product measure on each component and unit mass on $X^0$; write $s_j$ for $s_{n,j}$ on component $n$. The Fock realization in step 1.1 is a closed subspace of $L^2(\\Omega)$ consisting of symmetric components. Define $E(B)$ by multiplication by $\\mathbf1_{s^{-1}(B)}$. Since $s$ is invariant under permutations, this multiplier preserves the symmetric subspace. It is an orthogonal projection because its multiplier is real and idempotent; $E(\\varnothing)=0$, $E(\\mathbb R^d)=I$ and $E(B)E(C)=E(B\\cap C)$ follow pointwise. For disjoint $B_k$ with union $B$, the squared norm of $E(B)\\Psi-\\sum_{k=0}^N E(B_k)\\Psi$ is $$\\sum_{n\\ge0}\\int_{X^n} \\mathbf1_{s_n^{-1}(B\\setminus\\cup_{k=0}^N B_k)}|\\Psi_n|^2\\,d\\mu_n.$$ The indicators tend pointwise to zero and the integrand is bounded by $|\\Psi_n|^2$, whose total integral is $\\|\\Psi\\|^2$; dominated convergence [F6] gives norm convergence. Hence $E$ is a strongly countably additive PVM.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F6",
            "step 1.1",
            "given",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Fix a real measurable multiplier $f$ on $\\Omega$ that is invariant under coordinate permutations, and define $M_f\\Psi=f\\Psi$ on the symmetric domain $D(M_f)=\\{\\Psi:f\\Psi\\in L^2(\\Omega)\\}$. This domain is dense: the symmetric cutoffs $\\Psi\\mathbf1_{|f|\\le m}$ belong to it and converge in norm by dominated convergence. Reality of $f$ makes $M_f$ symmetric. If $\\Psi\\in D(M_f^*)$ and $K=M_f^*\\Psi$, then for each $m$ the vector $v_m=(f\\Psi-K)\\mathbf1_{|f|\\le m}$ is symmetric and lies in $D(M_f)$, since $f$ is bounded on its support. The adjoint identity gives $$0=\\langle M_fv_m,\\Psi\\rangle_2-\\langle v_m,K\\rangle_2 =\\int_{|f|\\le m}|f\\Psi-K|^2\\,d\\mu_\\Omega.$$ Taking the union over $m$ shows $f\\Psi=K$ almost everywhere, so $\\Psi\\in D(M_f)$ and $M_f^*\\Psi=M_f\\Psi$. Conversely, for every $\\Psi\\in D(M_f)$, reality of $f$ gives the adjoint identity against every domain vector, so $M_f^*\\Psi=M_f\\Psi$. Therefore $M_f$ is self-adjoint on its maximal multiplication domain. Apply this to $f=s_j$ to obtain each self-adjoint $H_j$, and to $f=\\theta\\cdot s$ to obtain $H_\\theta$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F4",
            "F6",
            "step 2.1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "For each $j$, the multiplier $s_j$ defines $H_j$ with the displayed component domain because the squared norm of $H_j\\Psi$ is exactly $\\sum_n\\int|s_{n,j}|^2|\\Psi_n|^2d\\mu_n$; the vacuum term is zero. For $\\theta$, the same calculation gives $D(H_\\theta)=\\{\\Psi:\\sum_n\\int|\\theta\\cdot s_n|^2|\\Psi_n|^2d\\mu_n<\\infty\\}$. The PVM from step 2.1 has coordinate functions $s_j$, so its indicators give the joint spectral projections for all the $H_j$ and its scalar-coordinate multipliers have these exact domains. Truncating a vector in any such domain after sector $N$ makes both its norm tail and operator-image tail tend to zero; hence $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}\\cap D(H_j)$ and $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}\\cap D(H_\\theta)$ are graph cores.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F5",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 3.1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "By [F6], $U_\\theta(t)$ is unitary and $U_\\theta(t+s)=U_\\theta(t)U_\\theta(s)$ on each component, including the vacuum where the phase is one. For any $\\Psi$, the squared norm of $U_\\theta(t)\\Psi-U_\\theta(t_0)\\Psi$ is the integral over $\\Omega$ of $|e^{it\\theta\\cdot s}-e^{it_0\\theta\\cdot s}|^2|\\Psi|^2$; the integrand tends pointwise to zero and is bounded by $4|\\Psi|^2$, so [F6] proves strong continuity. If $\\Psi\\in D(H_\\theta)$, the pointwise difference quotient tends to $i(\\theta\\cdot s)\\Psi$ and its squared error is bounded by $4|\\theta\\cdot s|^2|\\Psi|^2$; dominated convergence proves norm convergence. Conversely, if the difference quotient has a norm limit as $t\\to0$, take $t_m=1/m$. Fatou's lemma [F6] on the disjoint union gives $$\\sum_n\\int|\\theta\\cdot s_n|^2|\\Psi_n|^2d\\mu_n \\le\\liminf_m\\left\\|\\frac{U_\\theta(t_m)\\Psi-\\Psi}{t_m}\\right\\|^2<\\infty,$$ so $\\Psi\\in D(H_\\theta)$; the forward calculation identifies the limit as $iH_\\theta\\Psi$. This proves the exact derivative criterion and all claims.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "step 3.1",
            "step 4.1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
