      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Put $K=e^\\perp$ and $G=\\mathcal F_s(K)$ for a unit vector $e$. Since $H$ is separable, its continuous orthogonal projection $v\\mapsto v-\\langle e,v\\rangle_2e$ sends a countable dense subset of $H$ to a countable dense subset of $K$. Finite elementary tensors from that subset have dense span in each tensor power by [F8]; applying the bounded symmetrizer gives a countable dense subset of each $K_s^{(m)}$. Rational complex finite sums over the countably many sectors give a countable dense subset of $G$ consisting of finite-particle vectors. Gram–Schmidt, omitting zero residuals, therefore gives a finite or countable orthonormal basis $(u_j)_{j\\in J}$ of $G$ whose vectors are all finite-particle.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "F8",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "Map the one-mode basis $|n\\rangle=e^{\\otimes n}$ to $g_n$. The map $V:\\mathcal F_s(\\mathbb Ce)\\to L^2_2(\\mathbb R)$ is a linear unitary: the two displayed families are complete orthonormal families, and both pairings are second-variable-linear. Here $L^2_2$ has pairing $\\langle f,g\\rangle_2=\\int\\overline f g$; the core and self-adjointness of $Qf=xf$ transfer from [F4] by complex conjugation. The recurrence [F3] shows that $V\\,2^{-1/2}(a^\\dagger(e)+a(e))V^{-1}$ equals $Q$ on the finite Hermite span. Together with $J$ [F2], the full field for $h=re$ acts in $L^2_2(\\mathbb R)\\widehat\\otimes G$ as multiplication by $rx$ on the dense span of $g_n\\otimes u_j$.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F8",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-3",
          "claim": "Under the product-basis unitary of [F8], identify $L^2_2(\\mathbb R)\\widehat\\otimes G$ with $\\ell^2(J;L^2_2(\\mathbb R))$. Define $M_r(f_j)=(rx f_j)$ on $D(M_r)=\\{(f_j):\\sum_j\\int r^2x^2|f_j(x)|^2dx<\\infty\\}$. Its domain is dense because finite coordinates supported in $[-m,m]$ are dense. It is symmetric since $rx$ is real. If $g\\in D(M_r^*)$ and $k=M_r^*g$, test the adjoint identity in coordinate $j$ against $f_j=(rxg_j-k_j)\\mathbf1_{[-m,m]}$ and set all other coordinates to zero. This vector lies in $D(M_r)$ because $rx$ is bounded on $[-m,m]$; the identity then gives $0=\\int_{-m}^m|rxg_j-k_j|^2dx$. Letting $m\\to\\infty$ yields $rxg_j=k_j$ almost everywhere for each $j$, so $\\sum_j\\int r^2x^2|g_j|^2=\\sum_j\\|k_j\\|^2<\\infty$ and $g\\in D(M_r)$. Conversely, for $g\\in D(M_r)$, reality of $rx$ gives $\\langle M_rf,g\\rangle_2=\\langle f,M_rg\\rangle_2$ for every $f\\in D(M_r)$, hence $M_r^*g=M_rg$. Thus $M_r$ is self-adjoint with the displayed domain.",
          "step": "1.3",
          "inputs": [
            "F5",
            "F8",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-4",
          "claim": "The series defining $\\epsilon(z)$ converges in Fock norm because $\\|\\epsilon(z)\\|^2=\\sum_{n\\ge0}\\|z\\|^{2n}/n!=e^{\\|z\\|^2}$ [F6]. Its inner products are $\\langle\\epsilon(z),\\epsilon(w)\\rangle_2=e^{\\langle z,w\\rangle_2}$. These vectors are total: if $\\Psi=(\\Psi_n)$ is orthogonal to all of them, then for every $z$ and scalar $t$ the entire power series $\\langle\\Psi,\\epsilon(tz)\\rangle_2$ vanishes, so each coefficient $\\langle\\Psi_n,z^{\\otimes n}\\rangle_2$ is zero. For fixed $n$, the coefficient of $t_1\\cdots t_n$ in $\\langle\\Psi_n,(t_1z_1+\\cdots+t_nz_n)^{\\otimes n}\\rangle_2$ is $n!\\langle\\Psi_n,P_{s,n}(z_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes z_n)\\rangle_2$; it too vanishes. The symmetrized elementary tensors have dense span in $H_s^{(n)}$ by [F8], hence every $\\Psi_n=0$ and $\\Psi=0$.",
          "step": "1.4",
          "inputs": [
            "F6",
            "F8",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "The finite Hermite span is graph dense in $D(Q)$ by [F4]. The vectors $u_j$ from step 1.1 are finite-particle, so every $g_n\\otimes u_j$ belongs to the image of $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ under $J^{-1}$ and $V\\otimes I$. For any $(f_j)\\in D(M_r)$, truncating the countable coordinate sum converges in the graph norm of $M_r$ because both $\\sum_j\\|f_j\\|^2$ and $\\sum_j\\|rx f_j\\|^2$ converge. For each retained coordinate, if $r>0$ then $f_j\\in D(Q)$ and we approximate it in the graph norm of $Q$ by finite Hermite sums using [F4]; multiplying the graph component by the fixed scalar $r$ preserves convergence. If $r=0$, finite Hermite sums are dense in $L^2$ by [F3], and the operator graph norm is just the $L^2$ norm. Hence the image of $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ is graph dense in $D(M_r)$. Step 1.2 identifies the restriction with $\\phi_0(e)$, and scaling gives the same for $h=re$. Therefore $\\phi_0(h)$ is essentially self-adjoint and its closure is the self-adjoint $M_r$ transported through these unitaries, with exactly the domain stated. When $h=0$, it is the zero operator on the dense $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ and its closure is zero on all Fock space.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2",
            "step 1.3",
            "given"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "Write $\\lambda_h(z)=\\exp(-\\|h\\|^2/4+i\\langle h,z\\rangle_2/\\sqrt2)$. Using the exponential-vector inner product from step 1.4 and $\\langle z+ih/\\sqrt2,w+ih/\\sqrt2\\rangle_2$ $=\\langle z,w\\rangle_2+i\\langle z,h\\rangle_2/\\sqrt2 -i\\langle h,w\\rangle_2/\\sqrt2+\\|h\\|^2/2$, direct cancellation gives $\\langle\\lambda_h(z)\\epsilon(z+ih/\\sqrt2), \\lambda_h(w)\\epsilon(w+ih/\\sqrt2)\\rangle_2=e^{\\langle z,w\\rangle_2}$. Thus the formula in the Statement preserves inner products on the span of total exponential vectors and extends uniquely to an isometry $W(h)$. Substituting the formula twice, using $\\langle h,ik/\\sqrt2\\rangle_2=i\\langle h,k\\rangle_2/\\sqrt2$, gives the left-hand scalar $$\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{\\|h\\|^2+\\|k\\|^2}{4} +\\frac{i\\langle h+k,z\\rangle_2}{\\sqrt2}-\\frac12\\langle h,k\\rangle_2\\right).$$ Since $\\|h+k\\|^2=\\|h\\|^2+\\|k\\|^2+2\\operatorname{Re}\\langle h,k\\rangle_2$, this scalar is exactly the right-hand scalar below, including its Weyl phase: $$W(h)W(k)\\epsilon(z)=e^{-i\\operatorname{Im}\\langle h,k\\rangle_2/2} W(h+k)\\epsilon(z).$$ Taking $k=-h$ shows $W(-h)W(h)=W(h)W(-h)=I$, so each isometry is unitary and the Weyl identity holds on all Fock space. The vacuum case $z=0$ gives $\\langle\\Omega,W(h)\\Omega\\rangle_2=e^{-\\|h\\|^2/4}$.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F6",
            "step 1.4",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "The formula for $W(h)\\epsilon(z)$ is norm-continuous in $h$ for every $z$. Since the exponential vectors have dense span and $\\|W(h)\\|=1$, this implies strong continuity: approximate an arbitrary vector by a vector in that span and bound the two remaining errors by twice the approximation error. To identify its generator, write $h=re$, $z=\\alpha e+w$ with $w\\in e^\\perp$. Expanding the permutation average in the one-mode splitting [F2] gives $\\epsilon(z)=J(\\epsilon(\\alpha e)\\otimes\\epsilon(w))$. The absolutely convergent exponential series and their Cauchy product [F6] give $e^{2xs-s^2}=\\sum_{n\\ge0}c_n(x)s^n/n!$, where $c_n(x)=n!\\sum_{j=0}^{\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor}(-1)^j(2x)^{n-2j}/((n-2j)!j!)$. Here $c_0=1$ and $c_1=2x$. In $2xc_n-2nc_{n-1}$, reindex the second finite sum by $j\\mapsto j+1$; for each common power $(2x)^{n+1-2j}$, the combined numerator is $(n+1-2j)+2j=n+1$, giving $c_{n+1}=2xc_n-2nc_{n-1}$. The recurrence and initial values in [F3] therefore give $c_n=H_n$ by induction, so $\\sum_nH_n(x)s^n/n!=e^{2xs-s^2}$. Since $g_n=H_ng_0/\\sqrt{2^nn!}$, substituting $s=\\alpha/\\sqrt2$ shows that the Hermite series for $V\\epsilon(\\alpha e)$ converges pointwise to $g_0(x)e^{\\sqrt2\\alpha x-\\alpha^2/2}$. Let $S_N(x)=\\sum_{n=0}^N\\alpha^n g_n(x)/\\sqrt{n!}$. The $S_N$ also converge in $L^2$ by the Fock norm series. To identify these limits, choose a subsequence $S_{N_j}$ whose squared $L^2$ errors from the Hilbert-space limit are below $2^{-3j}$. The sets where $|S_{N_j}-V\\epsilon(\\alpha e)|>2^{-j}$ have measure at most $2^{-j}$, by integrating the squared error; their tail unions have measure at most $\\sum_{j\\ge J}2^{-j}\\to0$. Thus this subsequence converges almost everywhere to the $L^2$ limit, which must equal the pointwise generating-function limit. Multiplication by $e^{itrx}$ sends this function to $$e^{itr\\alpha/\\sqrt2-t^2r^2/4} g_0(x)e^{\\sqrt2(\\alpha+itr/\\sqrt2)x-(\\alpha+itr/\\sqrt2)^2/2},$$ which is exactly the image of $W(th)\\epsilon(z)$ under $J^{-1}$ and $V\\otimes I$. By totality, $W(th)$ is the coordinate multiplier $e^{itrx}$ for every real $t$. Its difference quotient converges on the displayed domain to $iM_r$: if $rx f_j\\in L^2$, [F6] gives $|(e^{itrx}-1)/t|\\le2r|x|$, so the squared error from $irx f_j$ is bounded by $9r^2x^2|f_j|^2$ and dominated convergence gives the norm limit; conversely, if a difference quotient converges, Fatou's lemma along $t_n=1/n$ gives $\\sum_j\\int r^2x^2|f_j|^2<\\infty$. Thus its exact generator domain is $D(M_r)$ and the derivative is $iM_r=i\\Phi(h)$; $h=0$ is immediate.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "step 1.3",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 1.4",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
