      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "For bosons, the ladder formulas [F1] give $a(e)a^\\dagger(e)|n\\rangle=(n+1)|n\\rangle$ and $a^\\dagger(e)a(e)|n\\rangle=n|n\\rangle$ for every $n\\ge0$, where the second identity at $n=0$ uses $a(e)\\Omega=0$. Their difference is $|n\\rangle$; linear extension proves $[a(e),a^\\dagger(e)]=I$ on the finite-particle core. For fermions, $c(e)\\Omega=0$, $c^\\dagger(e)\\Omega=|1\\rangle$, $c(e)|1\\rangle=\\Omega$, and $c^\\dagger(e)|1\\rangle=0$. Hence $\\{c(e),c^\\dagger(e)\\}$ fixes both basis vectors, while $c(e)^2=(c^\\dagger(e))^2=0$; these identities hold on the whole two-dimensional fermionic Fock space.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "The squared norm of $\\Psi$ is $\\sum_{n\\ge0}(n+1)^{-3}$, which is finite: on each block $2^k\\le n+1<2^{k+1}$ there are $2^k$ terms, each at most $2^{-3k}$, so the block sum is at most $2^{-2k}$ and the geometric series of these bounds converges. By [F3], $\\Psi\\in D(N)$ would require $\\sum_{n\\ge1}n^2/(n+1)^3<\\infty$. Since $n+1\\le2n$ for $n\\ge1$, $n^2/(n+1)^3\\ge1/(8n)$. The harmonic series diverges because each block $2^k\\le n<2^{k+1}$ has $2^k$ terms at least $2^{-(k+1)}$, so every block sum is at least $1/2$. Therefore $\\Psi\\notin D(N)$.",
